WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.280
How does a deterministic finite

2
00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.900
automaton decide whether to accept?

3
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.160
It reads a finite input

4
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.660
one symbol at a time

5
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.380
while remembering only its current

6
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.500
state. We will trace the

7
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.460
language of binary strings ending

8
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.300
in one and see exactly

9
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.820
when acceptance is tested.

10
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.160
A deterministic finite automaton has

11
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.460
five formal components. They are

12
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.460
the finite state set, alphabet,

13
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.740
transition function, start state and

14
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.400
accepting-state set. The transition function

15
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.300
assigns exactly one next state

16
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.020
to each state and alphabet-symbol

17
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:34.360
pair.

18
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.780
Start in the nonaccepting state

19
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.340
before reading anything. That state

20
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.760
means the prefix does not

21
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.580
end in one, which includes

22
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.680
the empty prefix. An empty

23
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.500
word is accepted precisely when

24
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.080
the start state itself is

25
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.620
accepting, so this machine rejects

26
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.040
epsilon.

27
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.280
Read the first one of

28
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.600
one zero one. The transition

29
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.160
takes the state token from

30
00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.160
the nonaccepting state into the

31
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.700
accepting state. The prefix now

32
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.220
ends in one, but we

33
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.500
have not yet decided the

34
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.980
entire input because two symbols

35
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.360
remain.

36
00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.400
Read zero next. The token

37
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.420
returns to the nonaccepting state

38
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.120
because the latest symbol is

39
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.820
now zero. This move shows

40
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.720
why visiting an accepting state

41
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.000
somewhere along the run is

42
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.380
not sufficient for acceptance.

43
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.780
Read the final one. The

44
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.780
token enters the accepting state

45
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.640
again, and this time there

46
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.820
is no unread input. The

47
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.580
whole word one zero one

48
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.360
is accepted because its final

49
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.400
state belongs to the accepting-state

50
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:35.760
set.

51
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.140
Now compare the shorter word

52
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.600
one zero. Its run also

53
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.260
visits an accepting state after

54
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.000
its first symbol, but ends

55
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.740
in the other state. Therefore

56
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.700
it is rejected, even though

57
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.600
a careless early-stop rule would

58
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.300
have accepted it.

59
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.560
To justify the machine generally,

60
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.120
state its memory invariant. After

61
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.380
every nonempty prefix, it is

62
00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.080
in the accepting state exactly

63
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.380
when the final symbol of

64
00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.520
that prefix is one. The

65
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.540
empty-prefix base case and the

66
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.440
two possible next symbols prove

67
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:07.960
this invariant by induction.

68
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.620
A complete transition table must

69
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.280
also cover inputs that are

70
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:13.560
not part of your favourite

71
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.040
example. If a state has

72
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.660
no intended useful continuation, add

73
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.040
a rejecting sink with a

74
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.060
transition for every alphabet symbol.

75
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.880
A missing transition in an

76
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.700
informal drawing is not permission

77
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.400
to invent a different deterministic

78
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:26.920
semantics.

79
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.620
Things to take note of.

80
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.320
Name all five components, identify

81
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.320
what each state remembers, and

82
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.220
trace every symbol before testing

83
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.160
acceptance. Check epsilon, a shortest

84
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.500
accepted word and a word

85
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.940
that enters an accepting state

86
00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.700
but later leaves it; those

87
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.960
cases expose remarkably many mistakes.
